# =============================================================================
# Qwen3.8-Flash-Next Q8_0 at 131072 -- the quality reference
# =============================================================================
# Same model and architecture as qwen38-flash-next-q4km-128k.env (read that one
# for the arch notes and the placement sweep). What changes is size, and it
# changes the whole economics:
#
#   Q4_K_M   110.957 GiB   33.9 GiB on the host
#   Q8_0     175.288 GiB   93.6 GiB on the host
#
# There is no fixed "loader ceiling" to appeal to -- RESULTS 51.3 is explicit
# that the 79.7 GiB the Q4 file puts on the card is where its tensors happen to
# land under -ncmoe 0, not a limit the loader enforces (this profile puts 81.7
# GiB there). What is true is simply that Q8_0 is 64 GiB larger, so nearly three
# times as much weight sits on the host, and this model is unusually punished for
# that: the Q4 sweep measured generation going 60.6 -> 128.9 t/s purely on how
# much of the expert set is resident, because 10 of 512 tiny experts per token
# is the access pattern PCIe handles worst.
#
# --- MEASURED 2026-09-03, sweep-bench, -c 131072 -----------------------------
#
#   -ncmoe  -ub    CUDA0     host    KV+compute   pp t/s   tg t/s
#      0    2048   126 971 requested         --   cudaMalloc OOM before the KV
#     13    2048    93 820   85 674          --   OOM allocating the 2224 KV
#     16    1024    86 170   93 324    2224+3 498  1124.7    42.0
#     17    2048    83 620   95 874    2224+6 996  2302.7    40.6   <-- shipped
#
# THE -ub CHOICE INVERTS RELATIVE TO THE Q4 PROFILE. There, generation was flat
# across -ub because the weight split did not move, so -ub was a free prefill
# knob. Here dropping to -ub 1024 buys one more resident expert layer, and that
# layer is worth +3.5 % generation while the halved micro-batch costs -51 %
# prefill. With ~94 GiB on the host either way, one layer cannot move the needle;
# the micro-batch can. So: the largest -ub that fits, and -ncmoe follows it.
#
# Against the Q4_K_M profile at the same context and the same -ub 2048:
#
#   Q4_K_M  -ncmoe 0    3486.3 pp / 128.9 tg
#   Q8_0    -ncmoe 17   2302.7 pp /  40.6 tg     -34 % pp, -68 % tg
#
# Q8_0 costs 3.2x the generation speed on this box. That is the price of the
# quality reference, and it is steep for one reason only: ~94 GiB of experts
# stream from host RAM on every token instead of ~34.
#
# HOST RAM: ~94 GiB of weights plus page cache against 244 GiB total, which is
# comfortable -- but see RESULTS 26.3, where a deep prefill at 262144 drove
# llama-server RSS to 203 GiB and was OOM-killed. That is why the 256k variant
# of this profile carries a warning the Q4 one does not.
#
#   ./serve.sh qwen38-flash-next-q8-128k
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/lmstudio-community/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GGUF/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-Q8_0-00001-of-*.gguf}"

: "${IK_ALIAS:=qwen38-flash-next-q8-128k}"
: "${IK_CTX:=131072}"

: "${IK_RTR:=0}"

# 17 is the floor at -ub 2048: 16 loads but leaves under 1 GiB after the compute
# buffer, and 13 dies allocating the KV cache. NOT transferable from the Q4
# profile, which runs 0.
: "${IK_FIT:=0}"
: "${IK_NCMOE:=17}"

# Largest micro-batch that fits at -ncmoe 17. See the table above for why this
# is worth more than the extra expert layer -ub 1024 would buy.
: "${IK_UBATCH:=2048}"
: "${IK_BATCH:=2048}"

# Kept at q8_0 for comparability with the Q4 profiles. If this profile is being
# used AS the quality reference, set both to f16 -- the cache is not the
# weights, but a Q8_0 reference deserves an unquantised one.
: "${IK_CTK:=q8_0}"
: "${IK_CTV:=q8_0}"

: "${IK_THREADS:=8}"
: "${IK_THREADS_BATCH:=24}"
